WHERE THE PER-STATION ANALYSIS WENT, AND WHY

This file says WHAT IT DOES. It used to also say what that MEANS, and Tony's
2026-08-23 offline report named the cost: the meaning was chronological prose
at the foot, so reading a station required scrolling between the analysis and
the station code, and there was nowhere for him to answer.

The meaning now lives in incant/designDocs as FrontierStations -- one entry
per station, each carrying the same eight attributes:

    gloss        the two-term hook
    purpose      what the station proves when it is green
    problem      the defect or hazard it addresses or exposes, by shorthand id
    workaround   what this file does today to get past it
    solution     the fix, or NEEDED plus what would generate one
    status       the lifecycle grade: baked, workaround, open, guess, ruled, remedy
    verdict      Tony's disposition -- the loud channel, where FIX THIS goes
    reviewed     Tony's prose

status and verdict are two attributes rather than one on purpose. A grade and
a disposition are different facts, and this project has paid four times for
putting two meanings on one channel.

The shorthand ids the analysis leans on -- bt32, bt34, bt35, bt37, H2, H4, H7,
RulingA, RulingD, RulingE, F31 -- are in the same file as Shorthand, with the
same attribute discipline. CLAUDE.md keeps the long form; that entry is the
index and carries the hook, the standing workaround and the STATUS.

TO LOOK EITHER UP: edit the lookup line at the foot of incant/lookup and run
it. lookuP(bt32 FSifElse) prints both. lookupIndex() prints every station and
every id, one line each.

